Kathy to my friends.

251
00:11:49,275 --> 00:11:50,177
We are friends.

252
00:11:50,210 --> 00:11:52,312
MIKE: Look, what do you want
from me?

253
00:11:52,345 --> 00:11:53,680
We are, Kathy.

254
00:11:53,713 --> 00:11:55,982
CROW:
Puller of ineffectual pranks.

255
00:11:56,015 --> 00:11:58,818
We were just on our way
to Prof. Salinas' place

256
00:11:58,852 --> 00:12:00,353
at the reservation.

257
00:12:00,387 --> 00:12:04,323
He's promised us
an authentic Indian summer.

258
00:12:04,357 --> 00:12:07,060
-Won't you join us?
-I'd like to if it's--

259
00:12:07,093 --> 00:12:08,928
if it's all right
with Johnny Longbow.

260
00:12:08,962 --> 00:12:10,663
It's all right with me.
No problem at all.

261
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:12,198
Just follow my car.

262
00:12:12,232 --> 00:12:14,667
SERVO: I'll explain the joke
more at lunch.

263
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:16,970
Why do call him
Johnny Longbow?

264
00:12:17,003 --> 00:12:19,740
That's his Indian name,
his tribal one.

265
00:12:19,773 --> 00:12:21,342
MIKE: Like Wahoo McDaniels.

266
00:12:21,375 --> 00:12:23,610
It translates "Warrior's bow

267
00:12:23,643 --> 00:12:26,012
that reaches long to its mark."

268
00:12:26,045 --> 00:12:27,447
CROW: Oh, is he seeing anyone?

269
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:31,418
Actually he handles a bow
like one of his ancestors.

270
00:12:31,452 --> 00:12:32,718
SERVO: I approve.

271
00:12:32,752 --> 00:12:34,220
The impact on the moon

272
00:12:34,253 --> 00:12:36,122
has set off a shower
of fragments

273
00:12:36,155 --> 00:12:38,825
mixed with pieces
of the asteroid.

274
00:12:38,858 --> 00:12:39,893
SERVO: More TV, anyone?

275
00:12:39,927 --> 00:12:41,160
Some of these
small fragments

276
00:12:41,193 --> 00:12:43,128
will enter Earth's
atmosphere tonight.

277
00:12:43,162 --> 00:12:45,398
But they will
undoubtedly explode

278
00:12:45,432 --> 00:12:48,133
into nothing more
than a harmless shower

279
00:12:48,167 --> 00:12:51,738
of tiny meteorites,
according to NASA station.

280
00:12:51,772 --> 00:12:53,807
The greatest concentration
for the

281
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:55,975
meteor shower
over the southwest region

282
00:12:56,008 --> 00:12:57,110
of the United States.

283
00:12:57,143 --> 00:12:58,978
This meteor shower will be,

284
00:12:59,012 --> 00:13:01,047
however, quite harmless

285
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:03,015
according to the statement
from NASA

286
00:13:03,048 --> 00:13:04,817
and likely to provide
nothing more

287
00:13:04,850 --> 00:13:07,787
than a spectacular
sight in the sky.

288
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:09,022
SERVO: I'm Kent Brockman.

289
00:13:09,055 --> 00:13:11,758
And that's the story
of the awesome collision

290
00:13:11,792 --> 00:13:12,958
on the moon today.

291
00:13:12,992 --> 00:13:16,330
This is Gary Kanin
from our news center

292
00:13:16,363 --> 00:13:17,930
in Albuquerque.

293
00:13:17,963 --> 00:13:20,000
CROW: Gary's a great guy.

294
00:13:21,702 --> 00:13:22,968
I didn't schedule
a meteor shower

295
00:13:23,002 --> 00:13:24,772
as part of the evening
entertainment

296
00:13:24,805 --> 00:13:26,740
at the reservation.

297
00:13:26,773 --> 00:13:28,207
That should be quite a sight.

298
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:30,477
BUDD: Oh, this is a great meal,
professor.

299
00:13:30,510 --> 00:13:31,912
JANET: That's a great stew.

300
00:13:31,945 --> 00:13:33,245
What's in it?

301
00:13:33,278 --> 00:13:35,515
-Oh, a lot of things.
-SERVO: Rattlesnake, Velveeta.

302
00:13:35,548 --> 00:13:38,452
Chicken, corn,
green peppers

303
00:13:38,485 --> 00:13:42,422
chili, onions.

304
00:13:42,455 --> 00:13:43,790
MIKE: Hair.

305
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:45,925
Well, it's an old recipe
around here.

306
00:13:45,958 --> 00:13:49,095
[ Music playing ]

307
00:13:52,198 --> 00:13:53,900
I'd like to get some
night shots of this area.

308
00:13:53,933 --> 00:13:55,435
SERVO: I'll give you a night
shot of an area.

309
00:13:55,468 --> 00:13:57,437
Well, if you don't mind me
tagging along.

310
00:13:57,470 --> 00:13:59,605
I know a few great spots.

311
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:01,273
But they're pretty far away.

312
00:14:01,307 --> 00:14:04,043
CROW: So we'd have
to go there naked.

313
00:14:08,247 --> 00:14:09,915
MIKE: Oh, thank you
for guiding me,

314
00:14:09,948 --> 00:14:12,385
or I would've walked
in any random direction.

315
00:14:13,585 --> 00:14:16,190
SERVO: Hey, they're going
for night shots at 12:00 noon.

316
00:14:16,223 --> 00:14:17,523
CROW: Yeah.

317
00:14:18,925 --> 00:14:20,760
[ Gasps ]
What's that?

318
00:14:20,793 --> 00:14:23,763
CROW: It's a Gran Fury,
about 1973.

319
00:14:23,797 --> 00:14:25,498
That is a lizard.

320
00:14:25,532 --> 00:14:27,167
SERVO: You girl.

321
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:29,837
Sorry, things like that
scare me.

322
00:14:29,870 --> 00:14:30,770
MIKE: Yes, I know.

323
00:14:30,803 --> 00:14:31,972
You get used to lizards here.

324
00:14:32,005 --> 00:14:33,272
They're quite common.

325
00:14:33,305 --> 00:14:35,908
That's why tribes in this area
have so many legends

326
00:14:35,942 --> 00:14:37,343
-about lizards.
-CROW: Ask me to tell one,

327
00:14:37,377 --> 00:14:41,247
-ask me tell one.
-BUDD: Like what, for instance?

328
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:45,118
Oh, such as the story of Lizard
and Coyote for instance.

329
00:14:45,152 --> 00:14:46,485
SERVO: Oh, boy, hunker down.

330
00:14:46,518 --> 00:14:49,222
In the days before
there were men on earth.

331
00:14:49,255 --> 00:14:50,590
Sounds like it's going to be

332
00:14:50,623 --> 00:14:52,492
the Navajo version of Genesis.

333
00:14:53,627 --> 00:14:56,730
Ignore him, professor.
I'd like to hear the story.

334
00:14:56,763 --> 00:14:58,698
CROW: I just want some peyote.

335
00:14:58,732 --> 00:15:01,333
They'll have no effect
on your grades.

336
00:15:01,367 --> 00:15:04,938
I don't expect it to.
I'd just like to hear it.

337
00:15:04,972 --> 00:15:06,807
Well, one day.

338
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:08,175
SERVO: [ Snores ]

339
00:15:08,208 --> 00:15:10,110
Before Man walked on earth,

340
00:15:11,077 --> 00:15:13,280
Lizard and Coyote
were having an argument

341
00:15:13,313 --> 00:15:16,148
about what shape
man would take.

342
00:15:16,182 --> 00:15:17,283
MIKE: They fought and died
in the end.

343
00:15:17,317 --> 00:15:19,352
-Lizard won the argument.
-CROW: And Coyote

344
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:21,253
-battled Roadrunner.
-They finally agreed

345
00:15:21,287 --> 00:15:22,822
that man's hands

346
00:15:22,855 --> 00:15:24,858
would be shaped like Lizard's,

347
00:15:24,892 --> 00:15:28,195
four fingers and a thumb.

348
00:15:28,828 --> 00:15:31,230
BUDD: Hey, that's right.

349
00:15:31,263 --> 00:15:33,065
JANET: Was that the end
of the story?

350
00:15:33,098 --> 00:15:34,733
-Not quite.
-SERVO: Not even close.

351
00:15:34,767 --> 00:15:36,668
Coyote drove a hard bargain.

352
00:15:36,702 --> 00:15:39,838
He agreed that man's hands
would be shaped like Lizard's

353
00:15:39,872 --> 00:15:43,877
rather than his paws,
but only, only...

354
00:15:43,910 --> 00:15:45,712
MIKE: If he retained
the film rights.

355
00:15:45,745 --> 00:15:48,213
...if man would be mortal.

356
00:15:48,247 --> 00:15:51,217
CROW: Uh-huh.
Do you know Billy Jack?

357
00:15:51,250 --> 00:15:53,218
And never again
try to be like Lizard.

358
00:15:53,252 --> 00:15:55,222
MIKE: And so
they called it maize.

359
00:15:56,388 --> 00:15:59,593
CROW: Stop talking,
Johnny Longbone.

360
00:16:01,393 --> 00:16:03,395
You can see
quite a distance up here.

361
00:16:03,428 --> 00:16:05,197
CROW: She's wearing
a frosted denim onesie.

362
00:16:05,230 --> 00:16:07,733
Although we've got a lot more
air pollution than we used to.

363
00:16:07,767 --> 00:16:10,002
MIKE: Since we ate
Johnny's stew.

364
00:16:10,035 --> 00:16:11,270
PAUL: That's Albuquerque
over there.

365
00:16:11,303 --> 00:16:12,672
SERVO: Before electricity.

366
00:16:12,705 --> 00:16:15,108
PAUL: And that road leads

367
00:16:15,142 --> 00:16:18,545
to Santa Fe, northeast,

368
00:16:18,578 --> 00:16:20,413
and the river's over there.

369
00:16:20,447 --> 00:16:23,382
-Paul, where are we exactly?
-SERVO: I'm telling you,

370
00:16:23,415 --> 00:16:25,285
what do you want,
longitude and latitude?

371
00:16:25,318 --> 00:16:28,588
I'm sorry, we're on the top
of Sandia Crest.

372
00:16:28,622 --> 00:16:31,692
It's 10,678 feet,

373
00:16:31,725 --> 00:16:33,025
up or down.

374
00:16:33,058 --> 00:16:34,693
CROW: Does that affect
my thighs?

375
00:16:34,727 --> 00:16:36,830
Depending on where you are

376
00:16:36,863 --> 00:16:38,030
and your point of view.

377
00:16:38,063 --> 00:16:40,133
CROW: Oh, I've never had
one of those.

378
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:43,068
Yeah, I like to come up here
at night.

379
00:16:43,102 --> 00:16:45,238
SERVO: And hock loogies
on passing cars.

380
00:16:45,272 --> 00:16:47,640
It's one of my favorite
places on earth.

381
00:16:47,673 --> 00:16:49,575
SERVO: After Branson.

382
00:16:49,608 --> 00:16:53,145
It's always so peaceful,

383
00:16:53,178 --> 00:16:55,082
-so quiet.
-CROW: So hot pants.

384
00:16:55,115 --> 00:16:56,883
Sorry, sorry.

385
00:16:56,917 --> 00:16:59,118
Somehow,
both the rest of my life.

386
00:16:59,152 --> 00:17:02,055
MIKE: That's a pretty low bar,
really, though.

387
00:17:04,423 --> 00:17:06,760
CROW: Well, they're not
making out yet.

388
00:17:08,493 --> 00:17:10,363
SERVO: Well, I guess
Wilt Chamberlain's in town

389
00:17:10,397 --> 00:17:12,032
or something.

390
00:17:13,098 --> 00:17:14,367
Right on schedule.

391
00:17:15,568 --> 00:17:18,172
SERVO: Johnny, are you
mouthing something?

392
00:17:20,773 --> 00:17:22,175
CROW: Tree.

393
00:17:22,208 --> 00:17:24,612
Paul, what's wrong?
What is it?

394
00:17:26,612 --> 00:17:28,415
CROW: They're heading
for the ovum.

395
00:17:32,985 --> 00:17:34,453
MIKE: Oh, yeah.
Yeah, I've used that

396
00:17:34,487 --> 00:17:36,690
-during meteor showers.
-CROW: Tease.

397
00:17:38,290 --> 00:17:40,427
Okay. Yeah.
Paul, the meteors are gone,

398
00:17:40,460 --> 00:17:43,197
-Paul.
-Paul, are you all right?

399
00:17:44,430 --> 00:17:46,332
-What happened?
-CROW: She gets off the ground

400
00:17:46,365 --> 00:17:48,368
saying "What happened" a lot.

401
00:17:48,402 --> 00:17:49,537
Meteorite,

402
00:17:50,270 --> 00:17:52,505
a lunar meteorite.

403
00:17:52,538 --> 00:17:53,240
A meteorite?

404
00:17:53,273 --> 00:17:54,840
CROW: A lunar meteorite?

405
00:17:54,873 --> 00:17:57,110
We heard about it
earlier on TV.

406
00:17:57,977 --> 00:17:59,412
Yes, but here?

407
00:18:00,847 --> 00:18:02,915
Well, we're right in the area
where they're due to fall.

408
00:18:02,948 --> 00:18:05,318
CROW: They have every right
to kill us.

409
00:18:05,352 --> 00:18:07,353
I guess we can consider
ourselves lucky.

410
00:18:07,387 --> 00:18:09,555
MIKE: I mean,
soon I'll be able to consider

411
00:18:09,588 --> 00:18:12,658
myself having
gotten lucky, right?

412
00:18:12,692 --> 00:18:14,193
What's the matter?

413
00:18:14,227 --> 00:18:15,662
You've got a scratch
or something

414
00:18:15,695 --> 00:18:17,297
-on your forehead.
-CROW: That's where my ear

415
00:18:17,330 --> 00:18:19,032
used to be.
I had it moved.

416
00:18:19,065 --> 00:18:20,600
I don't feel anything.

417
00:18:20,633 --> 00:18:22,368
Oh, no, don't touch it,
let me do that.

418
00:18:22,402 --> 00:18:24,003
CROW: Oh, words
every guy wants to hear.

419
00:18:24,037 --> 00:18:26,005
I must have bumped it
when we hit the dirt.

420
00:18:26,038 --> 00:18:28,140
MIKE: You don't have
to scrub it.

421
00:18:28,173 --> 00:18:30,110
Let me clean it up anyway.

422
00:18:31,143 --> 00:18:32,678
See, you were bleeding.

423
00:18:34,113 --> 00:18:35,248
That's nothing.

424
00:18:36,148 --> 00:18:37,818
SERVO: Let's celebrate
by littering.

425
00:18:38,918 --> 00:18:40,587
Hey, Kathy,

426
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:43,255
-look at that.
-MIKE: It's that tissue

427
00:18:43,288 --> 00:18:46,125
I just threw.
Hey, hi, tissue.

428
00:18:47,727 --> 00:18:49,595
SERVO: What ancient
civilization could've left

429
00:18:49,628 --> 00:18:51,698
this motorcycle here?

430
00:18:51,732 --> 00:18:55,202
[ Makes noise ]

431
00:18:55,235 --> 00:18:57,303
It'll make a great souvenir,

432
00:18:57,337 --> 00:18:59,138
my own personal moon rock.

433
00:18:59,172 --> 00:19:00,473
[ Gasps ] Moon rock?

434
00:19:00,507 --> 00:19:01,842
Oh, wow.

435
00:19:01,875 --> 00:19:03,008
PAUL: Did I say something wrong?

436
00:19:03,042 --> 00:19:04,710
No, but you just reminded me

437
00:19:04,743 --> 00:19:07,147
I'm supposed to go
into town tomorrow.

438
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:09,082
There's a NASA exhibit
at the university

439
00:19:09,115 --> 00:19:10,750
and I'm supposed to cover it.

440
00:19:10,783 --> 00:19:12,085
That's a great idea.

441
00:19:12,118 --> 00:19:13,118
SERVO: Um, without you.

442
00:19:13,152 --> 00:19:15,020
We'll go to the exhibit,

443
00:19:15,053 --> 00:19:16,322
then we'll have some supper.

444
00:19:16,355 --> 00:19:17,490
CROW: Then a dab of sex.

445
00:19:17,523 --> 00:19:19,192
Then we'll go out afterwards.

446
00:19:20,292 --> 00:19:23,062
And just in case the moon rock
hasn't cooled off.

447
00:19:23,095 --> 00:19:24,865
CROW: We'll just give it
a little time out.

448
00:19:28,467 --> 00:19:31,472
MIKE: Well, it's not cool
and now I have no plan.

449
00:19:32,938 --> 00:19:34,240
It's cool enough to travel now.

450
00:19:34,273 --> 00:19:36,008
CROW: It'd be cooler
if it played the bongos

451
00:19:36,042 --> 00:19:37,310
or wrote poetry.

452
00:19:42,182 --> 00:19:45,152
Have you got a first aid kit
in that bag?

453
00:19:45,185 --> 00:19:47,253
A few things. Why?

454
00:19:47,287 --> 00:19:49,988
I'm still worried
about that cut on your head.

455
00:19:50,022 --> 00:19:51,223
It could get infected.

456
00:19:51,257 --> 00:19:52,858
MIKE: If you keep pawing
at it, then, yeah.

457
00:19:52,892 --> 00:19:55,060
Look, I don't live far
from here

458
00:19:55,093 --> 00:19:56,963
and I've got
all kinds of antiseptic

459
00:19:56,997 --> 00:19:59,465
in my medicine cabinet
in my place.

460
00:19:59,498 --> 00:20:01,500
SERVO: Dozens,
no, hundreds of antiseptics.

461
00:20:01,533 --> 00:20:05,103
-Your place?
-My place.

462
00:20:05,137 --> 00:20:08,073
MIKE: I call it
'Antiseptic Manor.

463
00:20:08,107 --> 00:20:10,710
Fine, your place, then.

464
00:20:11,543 --> 00:20:14,615
CROW: Not his place,
fornicators.

465
00:20:16,315 --> 00:20:18,852
MIKE: New Puffs Plus
with Strontium-90.

466
00:20:23,188 --> 00:20:24,857
SERVO: I share
an effeciency apartment

467
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:26,493
with a bunch of other guys.

468
00:20:28,427 --> 00:20:30,697
CROW: I did that with my mind.

469
00:20:33,632 --> 00:20:36,870
MIKE: Okay. Now watch
how hard I hit the wall.

470
00:20:39,272 --> 00:20:40,607
SERVO: Should I leave
the bike running

471
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:42,808
so I can take you
right home afterwards?

472
00:20:46,812 --> 00:20:48,882
MIKE: Hey, he's got a nice
two-car living room.

473
00:20:48,915 --> 00:20:50,017
SERVO: Mm-hmm.

474
00:20:56,122 --> 00:20:57,857
Both look so furtive
and uncomfortable.

475
00:20:57,890 --> 00:20:58,758
It must be love.

476
00:20:59,992 --> 00:21:02,828
MIKE: That's one big French
press up there.

477
00:21:02,862 --> 00:21:04,530
I gather nobody's home.

478
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:06,967
My mother's in Europe,
she travels a lot.

479
00:21:08,668 --> 00:21:09,568
CROW: All this,

480
00:21:09,602 --> 00:21:11,772
and he lives with his mother?

481
00:21:11,805 --> 00:21:13,338
-Wow.
-SERVO: Hmm.

482
00:21:13,372 --> 00:21:15,675
Do you hang up
every centerfold you get?

483
00:21:19,545 --> 00:21:21,713
MIKE: Why do you keep a picture
of Army Archerd

484
00:21:21,747 --> 00:21:23,317
on your nightstand?

485
00:21:24,750 --> 00:21:28,988
♪ When will our eyes meet, ♪

486
00:21:29,022 --> 00:21:32,592
♪ when will I see? ♪

487
00:21:32,625 --> 00:21:34,662
SERVO: Where's your hair
coloring station?

488
00:21:37,563 --> 00:21:41,067
MIKE: Yup,
this is my corner fireplace.

489
00:21:44,603 --> 00:21:46,338
Now that the real Paul Carlson

490
00:21:46,372 --> 00:21:48,707
has stood up,
what do you think?

491
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:51,578
SERVO: You can sit back down.
I won't be needing his services.

492
00:21:51,612 --> 00:21:53,445
I think

493
00:21:53,478 --> 00:21:56,148
you spend a lot of your life
being lonely.

494
00:21:56,182 --> 00:21:58,483
MIKE: No, I got my chat rooms.

495
00:21:58,517 --> 00:22:01,287
When your parents are divorced,
you get used to it.

496
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:02,855
I'd like you to meet a friend
of mine.

497
00:22:02,888 --> 00:22:05,892
SERVO: Could you at least
kiss me first?

498
00:22:09,095 --> 00:22:10,763
Strom Thurmond.

499
00:22:11,898 --> 00:22:14,767
Hey, his name is Ty.

500
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:15,968
Ty?

501
00:22:16,002 --> 00:22:18,237
Short for Tyrannosaurus.

502
00:22:18,270 --> 00:22:20,840
Sounds like something
